Q: How to read excel file by row in Go language? A: <p>Excelize offers the ability to <em>stream data and read it in chunks</em>, saving RAM: The stream writer will try to use temporary files on disk to reduce the memory usage when in-memory chunks data are over 16MB.</p>
<p>Watch the tutorial: &quot;<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mzQhoVrKOz8" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Excelize Spreadsheet Library Tutorial - 3.1 Create a Million Cells Worksheet by Stream Writer</a>&quot; on YouTube, where you can see the complete code for the &quot;huge table&quot; (producing a 30MB .xlsx file in seconds, 102400 rows x 50 cols)</p>
<p>Below is the initial, simple, and <strong>working</strong> code.</p>
<pre><code>package main

import (
    &quot;fmt&quot;
    &quot;github.com/xuri/excelize/v2&quot; // $ go get github.com/xuri/excelize/v2 (20k stars)
    &quot;math/rand/v2&quot;
)

func main() {

    f := excelize.NewFile()
    defer func() {
        if err := f.Close(); err != nil {
            fmt.Println(err)
        }
    }()

    sw, err := f.NewStreamWriter(&quot;Sheet1&quot;)
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println(err)
        return
    }
    styleID, err := f.NewStyle(&amp;excelize.Style{Font: &amp;excelize.Font{Color: &quot;777777&quot;}})
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println(err)
        return
    }

    // struct with field Value, Formula, StyleID
    cell1 := excelize.Cell{Value: 3}                        // Cell A1
    cell2 := excelize.Cell{Value: 5}                        // Cell B1
    cell3 := excelize.Cell{Formula: &quot;SUM(A1,B1)&quot;}           // Cell C1
    cell4 := excelize.Cell{StyleID: styleID, Value: &quot;Data&quot;} // Cell D1

    cell5 := []excelize.RichTextRun{ // Cell E1
        {Text: &quot;Rich &quot;, Font: &amp;excelize.Font{Color: &quot;2354e8&quot;}},
        {Text: &quot;Text&quot;, Font: &amp;excelize.Font{Color: &quot;e83723&quot;}},
    }

    // slice of cells, type []any
    sli1 := []any{cell1, cell2, cell3, cell4, cell5}

    option1 := excelize.RowOpts{
        Height: 45,
        Hidden: false,
    }

    // Set cell value and cell formula for a worksheet with stream writer:
    err = sw.SetRow(&quot;A1&quot;, sli1, option1)
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println(err)
        return
    }

    // the big table, was huge, 102400x50, for now 102x20
    for rowID := 2; rowID &lt;= 102; rowID++ {
        row := make([]any, 20)
        for colID := 0; colID &lt; 20; colID++ {
            row[colID] = rand.IntN(640000) // random values
        }
        cell, err := excelize.CoordinatesToCellName(1, rowID)
        if err != nil {
            fmt.Println(err)
            break
        }
        if err := sw.SetRow(cell, row); err != nil {
            fmt.Println(err)
            break
        }
    }

    // final Flush() and SaveAs, with &quot;defer&quot; that closes and cleans up.
    if err := sw.Flush(); err != nil {
        fmt.Println(err)
        return
    }
    if err := f.SaveAs(&quot;Book1.xlsx&quot;); err != nil {
        fmt.Println(err)
    }
}

/*
    func (f *excelize.File) NewStreamWriter(sheet string) (*excelize.StreamWriter, error)

       NewStreamWriter returns stream writer struct by given worksheet name used for writing data
       on a new existing empty worksheet with large amounts of data. Note that after writing data
       with the stream writer for the worksheet, you must call the 'Flush' method to end the
       streaming writing process, ensure that the order of row numbers is ascending when set rows,
       and the normal mode functions and stream mode functions can not be work mixed to writing
       data on the worksheets. The stream writer will try to use temporary files on disk to
       reduce the memory usage when in-memory chunks data over 16MB, and you can't get cell value
       at this time. For example, set data for worksheet of size 102400 rows x 50 columns with
       numbers and style.

        The struct Cell can be used directly in StreamWriter.SetRow to specify a style and a value.
        The actual struct is excelize.Cell

        type Cell struct {
            StyleID int
            Formula string
            Value  any // interface{}
        }

    StreamWriter -&gt; write large amounts of data + flush() finale
    The stream writer will try to use temporary files on disk to reduce the memory usage
    when in-memory chunks data over 16MB
*/

</code></pre>
