<p>So there's the answer - the GLIBC <code>exit()</code>, which is invoked <strong>after</strong> <code>main()</code> exits, calls <code>_IO_cleanup</code>, which blocks on a mutex inside <code>_IO_flush_all</code>. <code>_IO_flush_all</code> is an internal GLIBC function to flush all C++ standard input/output streams.</p>
<p>As you've already found out, you can disable flushing <code>std::cin</code> by setting <code>std::cin.sync_with_stdio(false);</code>. Then the program would exit as expected, killing all detached threads.</p>
<p>So indeed, <code>std::thread::detach</code>, <em>when C++ standard library threads are implemented via POSIX threads (they don't have to be!)</em>, behaves exactly the same as <code>pthread_detach</code>.</p>
